Jia participates in a shooting practice with 4 bullets, among which one is a blank (a "blank" means a bullet without a projectile).  
(1) If Jia shoots only once, calculate the probability of the shot being a blank;  
(2) If Jia shoots a total of 3 times, calculate the probability of a blank appearing in these three shots;  
(3) If an equilateral triangle PQR with a side length of 10 is drawn on the target, and Jia, using live rounds, aims and randomly shoots at the area of triangle PQR, calculate the probability that all bullet holes are more than 1 unit away from the vertices of △PQR (ignoring the size of the bullet holes).

output:
(1) To calculate the probability of the shot being a blank when Jia shoots only once, we consider the ratio of blank bullets to total bullets. There is 1 blank bullet out of 4 total bullets.

\[
\text{Probability} = \frac{\text{Number of blank bullets}}{\text{Total number of bullets}} = \frac{1}{4}
\]

(2) To find the probability of a blank appearing in three shots, we first calculate the complementary probability (no blanks in three shots) and subtract it from 1. The total number of ways to choose 3 bullets out of 4 is 4, and the number of ways to choose 3 live bullets is 1.

\[
\text{Probability of no blanks} = \frac{1}{4}
\]
\[
\text{Probability of at least one blank} = 1 - \frac{1}{4} = \frac{3}{4}
\]

(3) To calculate the probability that a bullet hole is more than 1 unit away from all three vertices of an equilateral triangle with side length 10, we first find the total area of the triangle and the forbidden area near each vertex. The total area is \(25\sqrt{3}\). Each vertex has a 60-degree sector (π/3 radians) with radius 1, contributing a forbidden area of π/6 per vertex, totaling π/2.

\[
\text{Allowed area} = 25\sqrt{3} - \frac{\pi}{2}
\]
\[
\text{Probability} = \frac{\text{Allowed area}}{\text{Total area}} = \frac{25\sqrt{3} - \frac{\pi}{2}}{25\sqrt{3}} = 1 - \frac{\pi}{50\sqrt{3}}
\]

### Final Answer

(1) \boxed{\dfrac{1}{4}}  
(2) \boxed{\dfrac{3}{4}}  
(3) \boxed{1 - \dfrac{\pi}{50\sqrt{3}}}

